  "finding": "Investigated physically admissible coupled transfer on Candidate C2 (h=0.0010 mm <= ell/3.75, 2,300 quads) and C1 (h=0.0020 mm, 840 quads). Verified authentic constitutive parameters (ell=0.0075 mm, Gc=0.0027 kN/mm). Under frozen-damage mechanical equilibrium (residual <= 1.8e-13 kN), Candidate C2 achieves RF = 9.975 N (+27.05% vs donor 7.851 N) and fracture energy error +0.79% (<= 2%). However, under fully coupled PDAS equilibrium with physical history evolution (H* = max(H_mapped, psi0+)), the opening of the fully broken crack (d ~ 1.0) concentrates across the refined crack-band elements (height h=0.0010 mm), driving an undamaged strain surge (eps ~ 2.44, psi0+ = 847.88 kN/mm^2 vs H_mapped = 39.49 kN/mm^2), which triggers spurious damage diffusion into intact material and collapses equilibrated RF to 0.366 N (-95.34%). Alpha-sweep energy-consistent projection proves that reaction force continuity (+1.86% to +2.39%) can only be obtained by relaxing discrete mechanical equilibrium (residual 5.6e-4 kN). Therefore, simultaneous satisfaction of mechanical equilibrium (<= 1e-10 kN), reaction continuity (<= 5%), and un-capped physical history coupling is mathematically incompatible on C2, representing an intrinsic nonmatching crack-band discretization defect rather than an operator implementation failure.",
